from h import *

S = []
G = 'Switching'

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ VLANs, trunks, edge ports
qs = [
 Q('What does a VLAN do?', 'Splits one physical switch into separate Layer 2 broadcast domains', ['Joins several subnets into one broadcast domain', 'Encrypts traffic between switches', 'Routes traffic between subnets'],
   'Hosts in different VLANs can\'t talk at Layer 2. Each VLAN is usually its own IP subnet, and a router or Layer 3 switch routes between them.'),
 Q('Which commands put switch port Gi1/0/5 in VLAN 10 for a desktop PC?', 'switchport mode access, then switchport access vlan 10', ['switchport trunk native vlan 10', 'vlan 10, then interface vlan 10', 'switchport mode trunk, then switchport access vlan 10'],
   'Setting the mode to access stops it negotiating a trunk; the access VLAN command assigns the VLAN. If VLAN 10 doesn\'t exist, IOS creates it.'),
 Q('An IP phone and a PC share one switch port. Which configuration is correct?', 'switchport access vlan 10 and switchport voice vlan 20',
   ['switchport mode trunk with native VLAN 20', 'switchport access vlan 20 only', 'Two access VLAN commands, one per device'],
   'The PC\'s traffic is untagged in the access (data) VLAN. The phone learns the voice VLAN via CDP or LLDP-MED and tags its own traffic with VLAN 20.',
   card='How is a switch port set up for an IP phone with a PC behind it?'),
 Q('What does 802.1Q add to a frame sent over a trunk?', 'A 4-byte tag containing a 12-bit VLAN ID', ['A new IP header with the VLAN number', 'A 26-byte encapsulation header', 'Nothing; VLANs are identified by port number'],
   'The tag sits between the source MAC and the EtherType. 12 bits gives VLAN IDs up to 4094 (0 and 4095 are reserved).'),
 Q('What is special about the native VLAN on an 802.1Q trunk?', 'Its frames are sent untagged', ['It carries only management traffic', 'It is always VLAN 1 and cannot be changed', 'It is encrypted'],
   'The native VLAN (default 1) is untagged on the trunk. Best practice: change it to an unused VLAN and make it match on both ends.'),
 Q('What happens if the two ends of a trunk have different native VLANs?', 'Untagged traffic from one VLAN arrives in a different VLAN on the other switch, and CDP logs a native VLAN mismatch',
   ['The trunk will not come up', 'All VLANs are blocked', 'Nothing; native VLANs don\'t need to match'],
   'The trunk stays up, but frames for the native VLAN leak into the wrong VLAN. CDP spots it and logs %CDP-4-NATIVE_VLAN_MISMATCH.'),
 Q('Two switch ports are both set to "switchport mode dynamic auto". What does the link become?', 'An access link', ['A trunk', 'An EtherChannel', 'Err-disabled'],
   'Dynamic auto waits to be asked. If neither side asks, no trunk forms. Desirable + auto, desirable + desirable, or trunk on either side gives a trunk.'),
 Q('The trunk allows VLANs 10 and 20. An engineer enters "switchport trunk allowed vlan 30" to add VLAN 30. What happens?', 'The allowed list is replaced, so only VLAN 30 is allowed and VLANs 10 and 20 stop',
   ['VLAN 30 is added to 10 and 20', 'The command is rejected', 'All VLANs become allowed'],
   'Without the "add" keyword, the list is overwritten. Use "switchport trunk allowed vlan add 30". This mistake causes real outages.',
   card='What is the danger of "switchport trunk allowed vlan 30" on a working trunk?'),
 Q('Which command shows which VLANs are allowed and actively forwarding on each trunk?', 'show interfaces trunk', ['show vlan brief', 'show interfaces status', 'show spanning-tree summary'],
   'show interfaces trunk lists mode, encapsulation, native VLAN, allowed VLANs, and VLANs forwarding. Trunk ports don\'t appear in "show vlan brief".'),
 Q('A switch port connects to a lightweight AP that tunnels all client traffic to a wireless LAN controller with CAPWAP. How should the port be configured?', 'As an access port in the AP management VLAN',
   ['As a trunk carrying every client VLAN', 'As a routed port', 'As a voice VLAN port'],
   'In local mode the AP sends client traffic inside CAPWAP (UDP 5246 control, 5247 data) to the WLC, so the switch only needs the AP\'s own VLAN.'),
 Q('An autonomous AP (or a FlexConnect AP switching traffic locally) maps three SSIDs to VLANs 10, 20 and 30. How should its switch port be configured?', 'As an 802.1Q trunk allowing VLANs 10, 20 and 30',
   ['As an access port in VLAN 10', 'As an access port with a voice VLAN', 'With PortFast and BPDU guard only'],
   'When the AP itself puts client traffic onto VLANs, the port must carry them tagged. Usually the AP\'s management VLAN is the native VLAN.'),
 Q('Which PoE standard provides up to 30 W per port at the switch?', '802.3at (PoE+)', ['802.3af (PoE)', '802.3bt Type 4', '802.1Q'],
   '802.3af gives 15.4 W, 802.3at (PoE+) 30 W, 802.3bt (UPoE) 60 W or 90 W. Newer APs often need PoE+ or more for all their radios.'),
 Q('A new Wi-Fi 6 AP powers up but some radios stay disabled. The switch port supplies 15.4 W. What is the likely issue?', 'The AP needs more power than 802.3af provides',
   ['The port is in the wrong VLAN', 'The port should be a routed port', 'Spanning tree is blocking the port'],
   'Many APs boot in a reduced-power mode on 15.4 W. Check "show power inline" and use a PoE+ or UPoE port.'),
 Q('Which two settings suit a switch port for a single desktop, printer or IoT device?', ['Access mode in the correct VLAN', 'PortFast enabled'],
   ['Trunk mode with all VLANs allowed', 'DTP dynamic desirable', 'Root guard'],
   'End devices don\'t send BPDUs or need trunks. Access mode plus PortFast (with BPDU guard) is the standard edge configuration.',
   card='What is the standard configuration for a port facing a single end device?'),
 Q('Which command turns off DTP negotiation on a port that is already a static trunk?', 'switchport nonegotiate', ['no switchport', 'switchport mode dynamic auto', 'no cdp enable'],
   'With mode trunk plus nonegotiate, the port never sends DTP frames. That prevents a rogue device from negotiating a trunk.'),
]
