# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Rapid PVST+
qs = [
 Q('What problem does spanning tree prevent?', 'Layer 2 loops, which cause broadcast storms and MAC table instability', ['Routing loops between routers', 'IP address conflicts', 'Duplex mismatches'],
   'Ethernet frames have no TTL, so a looped broadcast circulates forever. STP blocks redundant paths until they\'re needed.'),
 Q('How is the root bridge chosen?', 'The switch with the lowest bridge ID (priority, then lowest MAC address) wins', ['The switch with the highest bridge ID', 'The switch with the most ports', 'The first switch powered on'],
   'Bridge ID = priority (default 32768 + VLAN ID) + MAC address. With default priorities, the oldest switch (lowest MAC) often wins by accident.'),
 Q('With Rapid PVST+ and default settings, what is a switch\'s bridge priority for VLAN 10?', '32778', ['32768', '4096', '10'],
   'The extended system ID adds the VLAN number to the base priority: 32768 + 10 = 32778.'),
 Q('Which command makes a switch the root bridge for VLAN 10, picking a priority lower than the current root?', 'spanning-tree vlan 10 root primary', ['spanning-tree vlan 10 priority 32768', 'spanning-tree root vlan 10', 'spanning-tree portfast'],
   'root primary sets 24576, or lower if needed to win. "root secondary" sets 28672 for the backup root. You can also set "spanning-tree vlan 10 priority 4096" directly.'),
 Q('Bridge priority must be set in multiples of which number?', '4096', ['1024', '256', '32768'],
   'The low 12 bits of the priority field hold the VLAN ID, so the configurable part moves in steps of 4096.'),
 Q('How does a non-root switch choose its root port?', 'The port with the lowest total path cost to the root bridge', ['The port with the highest bandwidth link to any switch', 'The lowest-numbered port', 'The port closest to the most hosts'],
   'Ties are broken by lowest neighbour bridge ID, then lowest neighbour port priority, then lowest neighbour port number.'),
 Q('What is the default STP (short-mode) cost of a 1 Gbps link?', '4', ['19', '2', '100'],
   '10 Mbps = 100, 100 Mbps = 19, 1 Gbps = 4, 10 Gbps = 2.'),
 Q('Which RSTP port role is a backup for the root port and becomes root port immediately if it fails?', 'Alternate port', ['Backup port', 'Designated port', 'Edge port'],
   'Alternate = another path to the root via a different switch. Backup = a redundant port to the same shared segment (rare, needs a hub).'),
 Q('Which three port states does RSTP use?', ['Discarding', 'Learning', 'Forwarding'], ['Blocking', 'Listening', 'Disabled'],
   'RSTP merges the old blocking, listening and disabled states into one: discarding. Learning fills the MAC table; forwarding passes traffic.',
   card='Name the three RSTP port states.'),
 Q('On the root bridge, which role do all active ports have?', 'Designated', ['Root', 'Alternate', 'Backup'],
   'The root has no root port; it is the root. Every segment it connects to has it as the designated (forwarding) bridge.'),
 Q('What does PortFast do?', 'Moves an edge port straight to forwarding, skipping the learning delay', ['Increases the port speed', 'Prevents the port from becoming root', 'Disables spanning tree on the whole switch'],
   'Hosts get connectivity immediately (no DHCP timeouts at boot). Only use it on ports to end devices, never to switches.'),
 Q('A user plugs a small switch into a port with PortFast and BPDU guard. What happens?', 'The port goes err-disabled as soon as it receives a BPDU', ['The port becomes a trunk', 'The new switch becomes root', 'Nothing; PortFast ignores BPDUs'],
   'BPDU guard says "this port should never see a BPDU". Recover with shutdown/no shutdown or errdisable recovery.'),
 Q('Where should root guard be applied, and what does it do?', 'On designated ports toward switches that must never become root; a superior BPDU puts the port in root-inconsistent (blocking) state',
   ['On the root port; it makes the switch root', 'On edge ports; it err-disables them', 'On all ports; it disables STP'],
   'Root guard protects your chosen root placement. The port recovers by itself when the superior BPDUs stop.',
   card='What does root guard do, and where does it go?'),
 Q('What does loop guard protect against?', 'A non-designated port moving to forwarding when it stops receiving BPDUs, such as on a unidirectional link',
   ['Users connecting switches to edge ports', 'Another switch becoming root', 'Broadcast storms from hosts'],
   'If an alternate or root port stops hearing BPDUs, plain STP would assume the path is clear and forward, creating a loop. Loop guard puts it in loop-inconsistent state instead.'),
 Q('Which command enables Rapid PVST+?', 'spanning-tree mode rapid-pvst', ['spanning-tree mode pvst', 'spanning-tree rapid enable', 'spanning-tree mode mst'],
   'Rapid PVST+ runs one RSTP instance per VLAN. Older PVST+ uses 802.1D timers (30–50 s to converge).'),
 Q('Which command enables PortFast on every access port by default?', 'spanning-tree portfast default', ['spanning-tree portfast trunk', 'spanning-tree bpduguard default', 'portfast all'],
   'Global "portfast default" applies to non-trunk ports. "spanning-tree portfast bpduguard default" adds BPDU guard to every PortFast port.'),
]
L = lesson(18,
 'Redundant links between switches are good until they form a loop. Spanning tree prevents that by blocking just enough ports. This lesson covers how the root is chosen, how ports get their roles, Rapid PVST+, and the protection features v2.0 lists.',
 [
  ('Why loops are deadly', [
    P('Ethernet frames have no hop count. A broadcast sent into a loop circulates forever, multiplying at every switch: a broadcast storm. Switches also see the same MAC address arriving on different ports and their tables thrash.'),
    P('Spanning tree (STP) solves this by electing a root bridge and blocking ports so that exactly one path exists from every switch to the root.'),
    CK(0)]),
  ('Electing the root bridge', [
    P('Every switch has a bridge ID: priority + MAC address. The lowest bridge ID wins.'),
    UL('Default priority 32768, plus the VLAN number (extended system ID). VLAN 10 → 32778.', 'Priority is set in steps of 4096.',
       'spanning-tree vlan 10 root primary → 24576 (or lower to win). root secondary → 28672.', 'Choose the root on purpose: normally a distribution or core switch.'),
    TIP('Ties on priority are broken by the lowest MAC, which often means the oldest switch wins. Always set the root deliberately.'),
    CK(1, 2, 3, 4)]),
  ('Port roles', [
    UL('Root port (one per non-root switch): lowest total cost to the root.', 'Designated port (one per segment): the port on the switch with the lowest cost to the root for that link. Forwards.',
       'Alternate port: another way to the root; discarding, ready to take over.', 'Backup port: spare port onto the same shared segment (rare).', 'All active ports on the root bridge are designated.'),
    TB(['Speed', 'Cost (short)'], ['10 Mbps', '100'], ['100 Mbps', '19'], ['1 Gbps', '4'], ['10 Gbps', '2']),
    DIA(svg(360, 180, 'Root bridge SW1; SW2 and SW3 root ports point to SW1; SW3 alternate port toward SW2 is discarding',
      switch(180, 30, 'SW1 · root', below=False), switch(80, 120), switch(280, 120), txt(50, 124, 'SW2', 'd-t', 'end'), txt(310, 124, 'SW3', 'd-t', 'start'),
      ln(170, 39, 90, 111), ln(190, 39, 270, 111), ln(95, 120, 265, 120, 'd-ln d-dash'),
      txt(152, 58, 'D', 'd-s'), txt(100, 98, 'R', 'd-s'), txt(208, 58, 'D', 'd-s'), txt(260, 98, 'R', 'd-s'),
      txt(108, 138, 'D', 'd-s'), txt(250, 138, 'Alt', 'd-s'), '<path class="d-blk" d="M246 114l8 12M254 114l-8 12"/>', txt(180, 168, 'R root · D designated · Alt alternate (discarding)', 'd-s'))),
    P('Root port tie-breakers, in order: lowest cost, lowest neighbour bridge ID, lowest neighbour port priority, lowest neighbour port number.'),
    CK(5, 6, 7, 9)]),
  ('Rapid PVST+', [
    P('Rapid PVST+ is Cisco\'s per-VLAN version of RSTP (802.1w). Each VLAN has its own tree, so you can make SW1 root for some VLANs and SW2 for others to share the load.'),
    TB(['RSTP state', 'Learns MACs', 'Forwards'], ['Discarding', 'No', 'No'], ['Learning', 'Yes', 'No'], ['Forwarding', 'Yes', 'Yes']),
    P('RSTP converges in about a second on point-to-point links because switches use a proposal/agreement handshake instead of waiting for timers. Enable it with "spanning-tree mode rapid-pvst".'),
    CK(8, 14)]),
  ('PortFast and the guards', [
    TB(['Feature', 'Goes on', 'Does'], ['PortFast', 'Edge ports (hosts)', 'Forward immediately'], ['BPDU guard', 'Edge ports', 'Err-disable the port if a BPDU arrives'],
       ['Root guard', 'Designated ports toward switches that must not be root', 'Block (root-inconsistent) if a superior BPDU arrives; auto-recovers'],
       ['Loop guard', 'Root and alternate ports', 'Block (loop-inconsistent) if BPDUs stop arriving']),
    UL('Per port: spanning-tree portfast, spanning-tree bpduguard enable, spanning-tree guard root, spanning-tree guard loop.', 'Globally: spanning-tree portfast default, spanning-tree portfast bpduguard default.'),
    TIP('Remember the pairs: BPDU guard → err-disabled. Root guard → root-inconsistent. Loop guard → loop-inconsistent.'),
    CK(10, 11, 12, 13, 15)]),
 ],
 [('2.5.a', 'Root port, root bridge (primary/secondary) and other port names'), ('2.5.b', 'Port states and roles'), ('2.5.c', 'PortFast'), ('2.5.d', 'Root guard, loop guard and BPDU guard')])
S.append(sec('Rapid PVST+ Spanning Tree', 'Rapid PVST+', G, '', L, qs))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ CDP/LLDP + troubleshooting tools
qs = [
 Q('Which statement compares CDP and LLDP correctly?', 'CDP is Cisco proprietary and on by default; LLDP is an IEEE standard (802.1AB) and off by default on Cisco IOS',
   ['Both are IEEE standards', 'LLDP is Cisco proprietary', 'CDP works only on routers'],
   'Use LLDP for multi-vendor networks. Enable it with "lldp run".'),
 Q('Which command shows a neighbour\'s IP address and software version using CDP?', 'show cdp neighbors detail', ['show cdp neighbors', 'show cdp interface', 'show lldp'],
   'The plain command shows device ID, local interface, holdtime, capability, platform and port ID. "detail" (or "show cdp entry *") adds IP address and IOS version.'),
 Q('A network diagram says SW1 Gi1/0/24 connects to SW2 Gi1/0/1. On SW1, "show cdp neighbors" shows Device ID SW3 on Local Intrf Gi1/0/24, Port ID Gi1/0/1. What should you conclude?', 'The documentation is wrong or the cable was moved: Gi1/0/24 connects to SW3, not SW2',
   ['CDP is broken', 'SW2 has been renamed SW3, so nothing is wrong', 'The diagram is correct; CDP shows the remote port first'],
   'CDP and LLDP report the real neighbour on each port. Use them to check the diagram and fix whichever is wrong.'),
 Q('How often does CDP send advertisements by default, and how long is the holdtime?', 'Every 60 seconds, holdtime 180 seconds', ['Every 30 seconds, holdtime 120 seconds', 'Every 10 seconds, holdtime 40 seconds', 'Every 2 seconds, holdtime 6 seconds'],
   'CDP: 60/180. LLDP: 30/120.'),
 Q('Which command disables CDP on a single interface, such as one facing the internet?', 'no cdp enable', ['no cdp run', 'cdp disable', 'no lldp run'],
   '"no cdp run" turns it off for the whole device. Per interface, "no cdp enable". For LLDP: "no lldp transmit" and "no lldp receive".'),
 Q('In extended ping, why would you set the source interface to the router\'s LAN interface?', 'To test that remote routers have a route back to the LAN subnet, not just to the link address',
   ['To make the ping faster', 'To use a bigger packet size', 'Because standard ping cannot reach remote networks'],
   'A normal ping sources from the outgoing interface. If the remote end has no return route to your LAN, a sourced extended ping exposes it.'),
 Q('A ping from a router shows "U.U.U". What does "U" mean?', 'A device sent back an ICMP destination unreachable', ['The reply arrived', 'The request timed out', 'The packet was too big'],
   '"!" = reply received, "." = timeout, "U" = unreachable (often an ACL or a missing route on a downstream router).'),
 Q('How does traceroute discover each hop?', 'It sends packets with an increasing TTL; each router that decrements TTL to zero returns ICMP time exceeded',
   ['It asks each router for its routing table', 'It uses CDP to find neighbours', 'It sends ARP requests along the path'],
   'TTL 1 reveals the first router, TTL 2 the second, and so on. Asterisks mean no reply from that hop within the timeout.'),
 Q('A traceroute shows hops 1 to 3, then only "* * *" to the end. What is the most likely meaning?', 'Something after hop 3 drops the traffic or its replies, such as a missing route or a firewall/ACL',
   ['The destination is reachable and fine', 'Hop 3 is the destination', 'The local default gateway is wrong'],
   'The path works up to hop 3. Look at hop 3\'s routing table and whatever is next.'),
 Q('Which command finds which switch port a device with MAC 0050.56a1.2b3c is connected to?', 'show mac address-table address 0050.56a1.2b3c', ['show arp 0050.56a1.2b3c', 'show cdp neighbors', 'show interfaces status'],
   'If the port shown is a trunk, go to the switch on the other end and repeat until you reach an access port.'),
 Q('Which command shows recent log messages, such as interfaces going up and down, on a Cisco device?', 'show logging', ['show history', 'show running-config', 'show version'],
   'show logging shows the buffered syslog messages. "terminal monitor" makes them appear live in an SSH session.'),
 Q('A packet capture shows a client sending TCP SYN to a server on port 443 repeatedly, with no reply. What does this suggest?', 'The SYN never reaches the server or the reply is blocked: a routing, ACL or firewall problem, or the server is down',
   ['The server refused the connection on that port', 'The TLS certificate is invalid', 'DNS is failing'],
   'If the server were up and the port closed, it would reply with RST. Silence points to the path or a filter.'),
 Q('In a capture, a server replies to a client\'s SYN with a TCP RST. What does that tell you?', 'The server is reachable but nothing is listening on that port',
   ['The packet was lost on the way', 'The path has a routing loop', 'The client has the wrong IP address'],
   'A reset is an active "no". The network path is fine; the service or port is the problem.'),
 Q('Which IOS feature captures packets on the device itself for later viewing or export to Wireshark?', 'Embedded Packet Capture (monitor capture)', ['CDP', 'NetFlow', 'IP SLA'],
   'EPC captures on the switch or router. SPAN copies traffic from ports or VLANs to another port where a capture laptop is attached.'),
 Q('Which command gives a quick summary of every interface\'s IP address and up/down status?', 'show ip interface brief', ['show interfaces', 'show ip route', 'show protocols detail'],
   'Usually the first command you run when troubleshooting a device.'),
]
